\begin{abstract}
Coding agents are routinely used on mainstream languages; their
capabilities on COBOL---a language running critical banking,
insurance, and government infrastructure---remain less understood.
We report a twelve-project empirical study in which two
general-purpose coding agents built non-trivial COBOL systems
from scratch, including two self-hosting COBOL-in-COBOL compilers,
a $\approx$1{,}630-Elo chess engine, a CDCL SAT solver within
$9\times$ of MiniSat, a Doom-like FPS, and a trust-suite-verified
compressor. The corpus spans $\approx$49{,}400 lines of working
COBOL, exercising up to 60 distinct constructs per project
across 10/10 language capability categories. Each deliverable
passes externally cross-checked oracles. A per-backlog rubric
averages 1.63/2 on correctness through reproducibility; cost was
75.7 active hours and \$6{,}446 upper-bound rack-rate.

The study enforces novelty (no prior COBOL implementation to
copy), $\times 2$ agent replication on identical specs, and
demanding per-task validation. Every subject produced working
executables. These results have implications for COBOL
maintenance and evolution, for migration pipelines that need
COBOL understanding as a translation prerequisite, and for the
community's benchmarks, which mostly do not measure COBOL
authoring at this scale.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
COBOL, coding agents, large language models, software engineering,
empirical study, capability evaluation, self-hosting compiler.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

